.TH CYAML_PARSE_MP 3 "2026-09-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cyaml_parse_mp, cyaml_parse \- parse a null-terminated YAML string into a DOM tree
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

cyaml cyaml_parse_mp(const char *yaml_str, char **err_str,
                     ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mp);
cyaml cyaml_parse(const char *yaml_str, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Parse a null-terminated YAML string and return the root DOM node.  The
parser discards comments (#) silently.  It resolves anchors (&name) and
aliases (*name) into independent deep clones.  It applies implicit
YAML 1.2 core schema typing to an unquoted scalar.

The parser resolves a tag (!tag, !!tag, !<verbatim>, or a shorthand
from a %TAG directive) and attaches it to its node.  cyaml_node_tag(3)
describes how a tag and the type of a node work together.  The parser
expands a "<<" mapping key as a merge key when that key is a plain,
untagged scalar, or when it carries the core merge tag (!!merge).
cyaml(7) gives the rules for the expansion and for precedence.  It
also gives the rules for anchors, aliases and tags.  The merged members take the
place of the "<<" entry in the member order of the mapping; see
cyaml_dictionary_first(3).  A dictionary keeps its members in the order
of the text, and a key that the text repeats keeps the place of its
first occurrence and the value of its last.  Three more cases are a parse
failure, exactly like any other input that is malformed.  They are a
tag that does not match the type of the content, a %TAG directive that
is malformed, and a merge-key source that is not valid.  !!null is the
one exception to the first of the three.  It forces CYAML_NULL on any
text, because there is no canonical "wrong" spelling for it.

SINGLE-DOCUMENT STREAMS
The input can hold exactly one document.  That document can have a
leading "---" marker, or no such marker.  It can also have a trailing
"..." marker, or no such marker.  The function then returns the root
node of that document directly.

MULTI-DOCUMENT STREAMS
The input can hold more than one document, with "---" markers between
them.  The function then collects every document root and returns one
CYAML_LIST node.  The elements of that list are the document roots, in
order.  The parser resets the anchor table between two documents.  An
anchor from one document therefore cannot be named by a later
document.  An empty document gives a CYAML_NULL element.  An example is
the document between two "---" markers that come one after the other.
A later document can omit its own "---" marker only when
the document just before it ended with an explicit "..." marker.  In
every other case that document must start with "---".  The parser
rejects bare content that comes directly after a document with no
"...", as a parse error.  A document that starts with its own "%YAML"
directive always needs an explicit "..." before it, even when it gives
its own "---" marker.
cyaml_serialize_stream(3) writes such a list back as a stream, one
document for each element.

cyaml_parse() is an inline wrapper that passes NULL for mp.

On a parse failure the function points *err_str at a null-terminated
error message, when err_str is not NULL.  The LIBRARY owns that
message.  Never free it.  It is not an allocation, and mp never sees
it.  The text stays readable until the next FAILING parse on the same
thread, which is the lifetime that strerror(3) gives.  The storage is
per-thread, so two threads that parse at the same time never overwrite
one another's message.  Copy the text to keep it longer.  On success
the function sets *err_str to NULL.  The return value always shows
success or failure, and *err_str never does.  Pass NULL for err_str to
discard the error details.

The message is printable ASCII whatever the input holds.  Where it
quotes text of the input (a scalar, a tag, an anchor or alias name, a
single character), it writes a byte from 0x20 to 0x7E as it is and any
other byte as <0xNN>, and it cuts a long quoted text short at a whole
byte with "...".  A raw control character, a terminal escape sequence
or a byte of a multi-byte UTF-8 sequence in the input therefore never
reaches a log that records the message.  A character that the input may
not hold at all is named as 0xNN, with its line and column.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
yaml_str   Null-terminated YAML input.
err_str    Out-parameter for the error string, or NULL to ignore it.
           The library owns that string and the caller never frees
           it.
mp         Custom allocator for all the nodes of the tree that this
           function builds, or NULL for the default allocator.
           The library keeps its own copy of the struct, so the caller
           may release or reuse the struct once this call returns. The
           functions that it names must stay callable until the last
           node built with them is destroyed. A process can use at most
           64 distinct sets of four functions; a call that would need a
           65th fails and returns NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
For an input with one document: the root cyaml node.
For an input with more than one document: a CYAML_LIST node that holds
every document root.
NULL on a parse failure, and also when the parser runs out of memory.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The caller owns the tree that this function gives.  The caller must
free it with cyaml_destroy().

The caller does NOT own the error string.  One rule covers the err and
err_str out-parameter of every module in this library: the library owns
the string and the caller never frees it.  See the note at the top of
common.h.

Check the type of the node with cyaml_type() to see whether the result
holds one document or more than one.

A plain, single-quoted or double-quoted scalar VALUE can cover more
than one physical line.  Each line break folds to one space.  A run of
blank lines folds to that many newlines instead.  These are the folding
rules of a folded block scalar.  An implicit mapping KEY is different.
It must always fit on one physical line, whatever its scalar style.  An
implicit key is written "key:", with no leading "?".  An explicit key
("? key") has no such restriction.  The parser canonicalizes a
sequence, mapping or flow collection that is a dictionary key directly
into its flow-style text.  It also resolves the core-schema type of a
scalar dictionary key before it stores that key, exactly as it does in
an ordinary value position.  For example, "~", "null" and an empty key
all canonicalize to the identical "null" string key.  "true", "True"
and "TRUE" give "true", and "false", "False" and "FALSE" give "false".
"yes", "no", "on" and "off" are strings under YAML 1.2 and keep their
own text.  An integer key gives its decimal text, so "0x10" gives
"16".  A float key gives the shortest decimal text that reads back as
the same double: "3.10" gives "3.1", "0.1" stays "0.1", and "1e300"
gives "1e+300".  ".inf", "-.inf" and ".nan" keep those spellings.  Both
zeros give "0", and a float with an integral value has no fractional
part, so the keys 1.0 and 1 are the same key.  Look such a key up by
its canonical text, for example cyaml_get(doc, "python.3\e\e.1") for
the key that "3.10:" wrote, or quote the key in the document to keep
its text as written.  This holds for an
implicit key, an explicit key, a flow dictionary key, and the
"key: value" shorthand of a flow sequence.  Every spelling of the same
value therefore collides on the same key, whatever the notation.  A
quoted key with no tag never obeys this rule.  The key text that the
parser stores for it is always exactly its own decoded content.

A tag on a key types that key exactly as it types a value, in every
key notation, and the parser then stores the canonical text of the
typed key.  "!!str 010:" and "!!str 10:" are two different keys,
\&'!!int "0x10":' is the key "16", and "!!int abc:" is a parse error.
The keys of this DOM are plain strings and not nodes, so the tag itself
is not kept.  An anchor on a tagged key names the typed node.

A node can carry at most one anchor and one tag, in either order.  An
alias can never carry either of them.  The parser reads a "<<" key
as a merge directive every time that key is a plain, untagged scalar,
and every time its tag is the core merge tag, tag:yaml.org,2002:merge
(!!merge, a %TAG shorthand of it, or its verbatim form), whether the
key is plain or quoted.  It does this in any mapping, whatever built that mapping.  There are
five forms.  The first is the implicit style of a block mapping
("<<: value").  The second is the explicit style of a block mapping
("? <<" and ": value").  The third is the implicit style of a flow
mapping ("{<<: value}").  The fourth is the explicit style of a flow
mapping ("{? <<: value}").  The fifth is the one-entry mapping that the
compact-mapping shorthand of a block or flow sequence gives
("- <<: value", "[<<: value]", "[? <<: value]").
That shorthand is the "key: value"
and "? key: value" notation.  An anchor on the key itself changes none
of this.  A "<<" that is quoted with no tag, or that carries any tag
other than the merge tag, is always an ordinary literal key, in every
one of those forms.

The input must be UTF-8 text made only of the printable characters of
YAML 1.2 section 5.1 (c-printable): TAB, LF, CR, 0x20 to 0x7E, NEL
(U+0085), U+00A0 to U+D7FF, U+E000 to U+FFFD and U+10000 to U+10FFFF.
The parser checks every byte of the stream before it parses anything:
scalars of every style, keys, comments, tags, anchors, directives and
the space between tokens.  It refuses the whole document at the first
byte that breaks the rule.  Refused are a byte sequence that is not
well-formed UTF-8 (a truncated sequence, a continuation byte with no
lead byte, an overlong form, an encoded surrogate, a code point above
U+10FFFF, a byte from 0xF5 to 0xFF), a C0 control other than TAB, LF
and CR, DEL, a C1 control (U+0080 to U+009F) other than NEL, and the
noncharacters U+FFFE and U+FFFF.  The error message names the defect,
shows the offending bytes as 0xNN, and gives the line and the column,
both counted from 1: LF, CR and CRLF each end a line, and the column
counts characters.  For example:

    raw control character 0x01 is not allowed in a YAML stream
    (line 3, column 7); write it as an escape sequence in a
    double-quoted scalar

    invalid UTF-8 in a YAML stream (line 1, column 4): overlong
    encoding (lead byte 0xc0)

The parser never repairs the input and never substitutes a character:
fix the data.  An escape sequence in a double-quoted scalar is the one
way to put such a character into a value: "\ex01", "\ex7f", "\ex80"
and "\euFFFE" are printable text that names it.  Two "\eu" escapes
that form a UTF-16 surrogate pair ("\euD83D\euDE00") name one
character, as in JSON.  An escape that names no character is a parse
error: a lone surrogate in a "\eu" escape, and a "\eU" value that is a
surrogate or above U+10FFFF.  Percent-escapes in a tag must decode to
well-formed UTF-8.

The parser rejects an escape that decodes to the null character
("\e0", "\ex00", "\eu0000", "\eU00000000"), and the percent-escaped
spelling of the same byte inside a tag ("%00").  The scalar value of a
node, its anchor name and its tag are each a plain null-terminated C
string, so a null byte inside one of them can never be represented.  A
raw null byte is outside c-printable; it can reach the parser only
through cyaml_parse_n(3) and cyaml_parse_n_mp(3), which take an
explicit length, because cyaml_parse(3) stops at the first one.

An implicit decimal, hex or octal integer literal whose value does not
fit in a signed 64-bit long long falls back to CYAML_FLOAT.  When the
text does not parse as a float either, it falls back to a plain
CYAML_STRING.  The parser never turns it silently into an integer that
wrapped around or that has the wrong sign.

Indentation is spaces only, as YAML 1.2 defines it.  A tab is
separation whitespace wherever the grammar allows separation: after a
\&'-', '?' or ':' indicator ("key:\etvalue"), after the spaces that indent
a line, inside a flow collection, after "---" or "...", between the
parts of a directive, and before a comment.  JSON indented with tabs
therefore parses.  A tab is refused where it would decide a
block-structural column: in front of a block mapping key or a block
sequence '-' on its line ("\eta: 1", "key:\et- a", "-\etb: c"), and as the
indentation of a block scalar.  Inside a scalar a tab is content.

Block-structured content is a mapping or a sequence.  It must start on
its own line after an explicit "---" marker, and it must not share that
line.  A scalar or a flow collection can share that line.

A UTF-8 byte order mark may stand in the prefix of a document: at the
start of the stream, at the start of the line of a later document
after a "..." or before a "---", and at the end of the stream.  A
prefix may hold several marks, with comment lines between them.  The
mark is not content, and a comment may follow it directly.  YAML 1.2
section 5.2 forbids a mark inside a document, so a mark at the start of
a line anywhere else is a parse error.  A concatenation of a file that
ends without a "..." and a file that starts with a mark therefore needs
a "---" after the mark.  A mark in the middle of a line is ordinary
content.

Every continuation line of a flow collection must be indented more
than the block mapping key or the "- " that holds the collection,
counting spaces only.  A line that starts with the closing ']' or '}'
is the one exception.  It may sit exactly at that indentation, after
spaces only, which closes the collection under the line that opened
it:

    key: [
      a,
      b
    ]

Any other content at that column, and a closing indicator further to
the left, is a parse error.

The parser rejects a document that nests mappings, sequences or
explicit keys more than 500 levels deep, with a parse error.  An
explicit worklist drives the parse, not recursion.  The call stack that
one parse needs is therefore a constant that does not grow with the
nesting depth of the document.  That limit bounds the work of a single
call, not the stack that the caller must give.  The parser also rejects
a non-scalar dictionary key whose canonicalized flow-YAML text is
larger than 64 KiB.  This second limit bounds a key that is itself a
mapping which holds another such key many levels deep.
Canonicalization re-quotes the already quoted text of each enclosing
level, so that text grows fast with the nesting depth.  The parser
builds that text only up to the limit, so a refused key costs at most
64 KiB of text whatever its shape, however many aliases it expands.
The limit applies to a key only: a flow collection in a value
position, anchored or tagged or not, has no length limit of its own.  No realistic
document that a person writes or a program generates reaches either
limit.

One parse call also rejects a document that needs more DOM nodes than
the larger of 4,000,000 and 4 for each byte of input.  The parse error
names the limit explicitly.

One parse call also rejects a document whose DOM would use more than
the larger of 256 MiB and 512 bytes for each byte of input.  An
ordinary document of any size stays well below both limits.
The count covers the node structs, their backing containers,
the text of every scalar, every tag and every dictionary key.  The
parse error again names this limit explicitly instead of a report of
out of memory.  This limit bounds what the node count alone cannot.  A
node is not a fixed amount of memory, and an alias deep-copies
everything that its anchor holds.  A small document whose bytes sit in
a few large anchored scalars can therefore expand without bound while
its node count stays low.

The nodes and bytes that a parse makes by copying what it already
built also count against a fixed alias expansion limit of 4,000,000
nodes and 256 MiB, which does not grow with the input.  A copy is the
anchored node that an anchor registers, the node that an alias
resolves to, a member that a merge key copies into its mapping, and the
canonical text of a key that is a sequence or a mapping.  These are
the only ways in which a document makes more DOM than its text
describes, so a nested-alias document meets this limit at the same
point whether or not a long comment pads it.  The parse error names
this limit too.

None of these limits applies to a tree that the caller builds directly
through the construction API outside a parse.

A float always uses '.' as its decimal point, whatever LC_NUMERIC the
process or the calling thread uses.  For the length of the call, the
calling thread runs in the "C" locale through uselocale(3), which
changes no other thread, and the call restores the locale of the
thread before it returns.  A custom allocator that the call invokes
runs inside that window.
.fi
